n\nStart with 149,744.\n\nAdd 400,000: 149,744 + 400,000 = 549,744.\n\nNow add the remaining 21,269 to 549,744:\n\n549,744 + 21,269:\n\nFirst add 20,000 to 549,744: 549,744 +20,000 =569,744.\n\nNow add 1,269 to 569,744:\n\n569,744 +1,000 =570,744.\n\n570,744 +269 =571,013.\n\nHmm, so why does the digit-wise addition give 570,113?\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. When adding the thousands place:\n\nIn the digit-wise approach, the thousands place for the first number is 9 (from 149,744's thousands digit is the fourth digit from the right: 1 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),7 (hundreds), etc.). The second number's thousands digit is 1 (from 421,269: 4 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),1 (thousands), etc.).\n\nSo adding 9 +1 =10 plus carryover 1 from the hundreds place gives 11. So write 1 and carryover1 to the next (ten thousands place). The ten thousands place is 4 (from first number) +2 (from second number) plus the carryover1: 4+2+1=7. So that's correct. So the thousands digit is 1, and the ten thousands digit is7. So the number is:\n\nHundred thousands:5 (1+4)\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:0 (from the hundreds place calculation)\n\nTens:1 (from the tens place)\n\nOnes:3.\n\nThus, 570,113.\n\nBut when breaking down, I got 571,013. Where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait in the breakdown:\n\nWhen I added 149,744 +421,269 by breaking into components:\n\n100,000 +400,000 =500,000.\n\nThen 40,000 +20,000 =60,000 → total 560,000.\n\nThen 9,000 +1,000 =10,000 → total 570,000.\n\nThen 700 +200=900 →570,900.\n\nThen 40 +60=100 →571,000.\n\nThen 4+9=13 →571,013.\n\nWait, but the thousands place in the first number is 9,000 and second is1,000. So 9,000+1,000=10,000, which is correct. But in the digit-wise addition, the thousands place after adding gives 1 (digit) and carryover1 to the ten thousands. So the total thousands contribution is 1,000 (from the digit) plus 10,000 (carryover to ten thousands). Wait but in the breakdown, the 10,000 is added as part of the thousands place? Hmm, perhaps the breakdown method is not accounting for the carryover properly.\n\nWait in the breakdown method, when adding 9,000 +1,000=10,000, that's correct. But in the digit method, the thousands digit is 1 and carryover1 to the ten thousands. So the total from thousands place is 1,000, and the carryover adds to the ten thousands. So the ten thousands place becomes 7, which includes that carryover. So total is:\n\nHundred thousands:5\n\nTen thousands:7 → 70,000\n\nThousands:1 →1,000\n\nHundreds:0 →0\n\nTens:1 →10\n\nOnes:3 →3.\n\nTotal:500,000 +70,000=570,000 +1,000=571,000? Wait no. Wait:\n\nWait 5 (hundred thousands) is 500,000.\n\n7 (ten thousands) is 70,000 → total so far 570,000.\n\nThen thousands place is1 →1,000 →571,000.\n\nThen hundreds is0, tens1, ones3 → total 571,000 + 0 +10 +3 =571,013.\n\nWait, but according to digit-wise calculation, the ten thousands digit is7, thousands is1, so 570,000 +1,000 =571,000. Then adding the hundreds, tens, ones: 0+10+3=13 → total 571,013.\n\nAh! So the digit-wise calculation actually gives the same as the breakdown method. But in my first digit-wise analysis I thought it was 570,113, which was a mistake. Let me re-express the digit-wise result:\n\nDigits from the addition:\n\nHundred thousands:5 (500,000)\n\nTen thousands:7 (70,000)\n\nThousands:1 (1,000)\n\nHundreds:0 (0)\n\nTens:1 (10)\n\nOnes:3 (3)\n\nSo total is 500,000 +70,000 =570,000 +1,000 =571,000 +10 +3 =571,013. Therefore, my earlier digit-wise breakdown was wrong in the first calculation because I miscounted the places.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 571,013.\n\nWait, now I'm really confused. Let me try a calculator-like approach.\n\nLet me compute 149,744 +421,269.\n\nFirst number:149,744.\n\nSecond number:421,269.\n\nAdding:\n\nStart with 149,744 +421,269.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n149,744\n\n+421,269\n\n=?\n\nLet me add the numbers step by step:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes digit:4 +9=13 → write 3, carryover1