So we need to write down 6 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 4 to the millions place. But since there are no higher digits, that carried-over 3 becomes a new digit at the beginning. .\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 7.\n\nTens: 3.\n\nHundreds: 9.\n\nThousands: 7.\n\nTen-thousands: 7.\n\nHundred-thousands: 2 (with a carryover of 6).\n\nThen the millions place: the carried-over 2.\n\nSo combining all these digits: 3 (million), 9 (hundred-thousand), 4 (ten-thousand), 0 (thousand), 3 (hundred), 1 (ten), 8 (one). Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me recount:.\n\nWait, let's do this step by step again carefully:.\n\nLet me write each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n8. Ones place: 0 + 3 = 97 → 2, carryover 8.\n\n6. Tens place: 6 (from 136059) + 4 (from 785151) + 4 (carry) = 1. So tens digit is 8, no carryover.\n\n8. Hundreds place: 1 (from first number) + 4 (from second) = 4. So 8, no carry.\n\n2. Thousands place: 4 + 3 = 6. So 2, no carry.\n\n6. Ten-thousands place: 7 (from first number's ten-thousands place) + 9 (from second number's ten-thousands place, since 594217 is 8 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), etc.) → 2+3=7. So 4.\n\n1. Hundred-thousands place: 3 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) = 28. So here, we write down 5 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 2 to the millions place.\n\nSince there's a carryover of 9 to the millions place, that becomes the next digit. So the total sum is 6 (million) 4 (hundred thousand) 4 (ten thousand) 2 (thousand) 1 (hundred) 6 (ten) 3 (one). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
