r 4 from the ten-thousands place. So 35 + 0 = 45. Therefore, the hundred-thousands place will be 8, and we need to carry over 7 to the millions place. Wait, but the original numbers don't have a digit in the millions place. So that carried-over 2 becomes the new digit in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right: the digits we've calculated are 7 (ones), 0 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 7 (thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 4 (hundred-thousands), and then the carried-over 9 in the millions place. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
