he hundreds place was 6 (from first) +8 (from second) =14, so carryover 1 to thousands.\n\nSo when adding the thousands place, it's 1 (from first) +4 (from second) +1 (carryover) =6. So thousands digit is 6.\n\nBut in the breakdown approach, when adding 14,807 to 581,691, the thousands digit of 581,691 is 1, and adding 4 (from 14,807's thousands place) gives 5, but there was a carryover from the hundreds place which is 1. Wait, but in the breakdown method, I didn't account for that carryover. Because in the breakdown method, I separated the addition into 10,000 +4,807, but the carryover from the hundreds place is part of the digit-by-digit process and wasn't considered in the breakdown.\n\nAh! That's the key. The breakdown method I used earlier didn't account for the carryover from the hundreds place when adding the thousands place. So when adding 14,807 to 581,691, the hundreds digit addition (6 +8) causes a carryover that must be added to the thousands place. But in the breakdown, I just added the numbers as separate components without considering the intermediate carryover.\n\nTherefore, the digit-by-digit method is more accurate because it properly accounts for all carryovers step by step. So the correct total is indeed 596,508.\n\nTherefore, after carefully going through each step and correcting my earlier mistake in the breakdown approach, the correct sum of 381,691 and 214,807 is 596,508.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method. Let's use another breakdown:\n\n381,691 + 214,807 = ?\n\nBreak both numbers into parts:\n\n381,691 = 300,000 + 80,000 + 1,000 + 600 + 90 +1\n\n214,807 = 200,000 +10,000 +4,000 +800 +0 +7\n\nNow add each part:\n\n300k +200k =500k\n\n80k +10k =90k → total so far 590k\n\n1k +4k =5k → total 595k\n\n600 +800 =1,400 → total 596,400\n\n90 +0 =90 → total 596,490\n\n1 +7 =8 → total 596,498.\n\nWait, so now this method gives 596,498 again, conflicting with digit-by-digit. There's inconsistency here. Where is the problem?\n\nAh! Because 