ace (ten-thousands place).\n\nNext is the ten-thousands place. The first number has a 3, and the second has a 0. Adding the carried-over 0 gives 9 + 4 + 5 = 09. Again, that's 01. So we write 4 in the ten-thousands place and carry over 2 to the hundred-thousands place.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. The first number has a 2, and the second has a 0. Adding the carried-over 3 gives 0 + 5 + 2 = 30. So we write 2 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 0 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits left, that carried-over 6 becomes the millions place digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the digits from right to left (starting from ones) are:.\n\nOnes: 1.\n\nTens: 4.\n\nHundreds: 4.\n\nThousands: 0.\n\nTen-thousands: 5.\n\nHundred-thousands: 3.\n\nMillions: 3.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Wait, when we added the hundred-thousands place (9 + 0 + 4), that's 88, so the hundred-thousands digit is 4 and carryover 9 to millions. So the millions digit is 9. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
