er 1 to the millions place.\n\nSo the total would be 1 (million) 1 (hundred thousand) 3 (ten thousand) 8 (thousand) 2 (hundred) 0 (ten) 7 (one). So 1,138,207?\n\nWait, but the first number is 212,963 and second is 925,244. Let me add them as whole numbers:\n\n212,963 + 925,244.\n\nLet me compute 200,000 +900,000 =1,100,000.\n\n12,000 +25,000=37,000 → total so far 1,137,000.\n\nThen 963 +244=1,207 → total is 1,137,000 +1,207 =1,138,207. Yes that matches.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1,138,207.\n\nWait but the user\'s question was "What is the sum of 212963 and 925244?" So the numbers are 212963 and 925244. So yes, the correct sum is 1,138,207. So the reasoning steps should follow that.\n\nWait, but in the initial problem, the user\'s question has the numbers as 212963 and 925244. So let me recheck the digit positions again to make sure I didn\'t flip any numbers.\n\n212963:\n\nDigit places from right: ones (3), tens (6), hundreds (9), thousands (2), ten-thousands (1), hundred-thousands (2).\n\n925244:\n\nOnes (4), tens (4), hundreds (2), thousands (5), ten-thousands (2), hundred-thousands (9).\n\nAdding each column:\n\nOnes: 3+4=7.\n\nTens:6+4=10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:9+2=11 +1=12 → 2, carryover1.\n\nThousands:2 (from first) +5 (from second) =7 +1=8 → no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands:1 +2=3.\n\nHundred-thousands:2 +9=11 → so 1 in that place and carryover 1 to the millions. So total is 1,138,207.\n\nYes. So the answer is correct.\n']
