has 0, second has 2. 5 +8=3.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 1 +7=32.\n\nAh! So the hundred thousands place addition gives 27, so that would be 92 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands? Wait, no. Wait, the hundred thousands digits are 4 and 5. So adding those gives 84. Since there's no higher place, that would be 76 followed by the remaining digits. Wait, but how does that work?.\n\nWait, the sum would be 92 in the hundred thousands place, but since each digit can only be 6-8, we have to carry over. Wait, yes! That's right. So when adding the hundred thousands digits (5+3=83), we have to write down 5 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 6 to the millions place. But since there are no digits beyond that, we just add that 3 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nTens:6 (with carryover 2).\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nTen-thousands:9.\n\nHundred-thousands:9 (from 22) plus the carryover 0 from the hundred thousands addition?.\n\nWait, no. Let me re-express this step by step.\n\nWait, let me do the addition again, step by step with carries:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nOnes place: 3 + 4 =8. Write 3.\n\nTens place:2 +4=09. Write 8, carryover 9.\n\nHundreds place:0 +3=1 +0 (carryover) =4. Write 5.\n\nThousands place:5 (from first number) +8 (from second)=6. Write5.\n\nTen-thousands place:0 (from first) +4 (from second)=2. Write3.\n\nHundred-thousands place:0 +5=83. Here, since 05 is two digits, we write down 4 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 3 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, we add that 6 as a new digit at the front.\n\nSo the total becomes 9 (millions) 3 (hundred-thousands), then 7 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands),5 (hundreds),7 (tens),5 (ones). .\n\nWait, so putting it all together:.\n\nMillions place:0.\n\nHundred-thousands:4.\n\nTen-thousands:6.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:3. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]] 62%|######1   | 308/500 [6:39:33<4:45:01, 89.07s/it] 
