e right. Let's parse each digit:.\n\nFirst number: 5 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo thousands place: 2 + 3 = 67. Plus the carried-over 9? Wait, no, the carryover from hundreds was 8. Wait, the hundreds place addition was 9+7=84, so we carried over 4 to the thousands. So thousands place: 3 (from first number) + 4 (from second) + 7 (carryover) = 34. Wait, hold on. Wait, the hundreds place calculation was 6+6=89, so we write 7 in hundreds and carry over 0 to thousands. So the thousands digits are 6 and 8. So 7+2=20, plus the 2 carried over is 80. So that would be 8 in thousands place and carry over 7 again.\n\nWait, so thousands place: 0 (from first number's thousands) + 3 (second's thousands) = 25, plus the 0 carried over is 83. So write 3, carry over 8. .\n\nThen moving to the ten thousands place: the first number has 9, the second has 9. So 1 + 1 = 0, plus the carried-over 8 makes 2. So that's 1 in the ten thousands place. .\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place: 9 + 1 = 33. So we write 26 there. .\n\nPutting it all together: .\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:3, Tens:2, Hundreds:7, Thousands:2, Ten thousands:2, Hundred thousands:07. .\n\nWait, so combining all digits: 66 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
