e there's no millions place in the original numbers, the 1 is added as a new digit at the front.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nMillions place:1\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nThousands:0\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:2\n\nOnes:7\n\nSo the total is 1,650,727.\n\nWait, but let me verify with actual addition:\n\n733,321\n\n+917,406\n\n= ?\n\nLet me compute this directly:\n\n700,000 + 900,000 =1,600,000\n\n33,000 +17,000 =50,000\n\n321 +406 =727\n\nAdding those together: 1,600,000 +50,000 =1,650,000; plus 727 gives 1,650,727. So that's correct. Therefore, the answer is 1,650,727.\n\nBut the problem is presented as <<number1>> and <<number2>> which were 733321 and 917406. So the correct sum is 1,650,727.\n\nWait, but in my first reasoning above, when I thought of the thousands place, I had to carry over 1, but maybe I messed up the digits. Anyway, the correct answer is 1,650,727. So the reasoning should reflect that.\n\nSo, step by step:\n\nOnes:1+6=7\n\nTens:2+0=2\n\nHundreds:3+4=7\n\nThousands:3+7=10 (0 with carryover 1)\n\nTen thousands:3+1 +1=5\n\nHundred thousands:7+9=16 (6 with carryover 1)\n\nMillions place:1\n\nThus, the digits are 1 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands),7 (hundreds),2 (tens),7 (ones). So 1,650,727.\n\nOkay, that's correct.\n"]
