ds), 4 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\n048,779 is 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo when adding the thousands place: the first number has 3 (thousands place) and the second has 1 (thousands place). So 9 +0 =02. So yes, 1 in thousands and carryover 9 to the next higher place (ten thousands).\n\nContinuing:.\n\n0. **Ten thousands place:** The first number has 4 (ten thousands), the second has 7 (ten thousands). Adding those: 0 + 0 = 63. Plus the carried-over 4 makes 00. So we put 9 in the ten thousands place and carry over 0 to the hundred thousands.\n\n9. **Hundred thousands place:** The first number has 2, the second has 1. 6 +1 =3. Add the carried-over 7 to get 48. So that would be 4 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 9 to the millions place. Wait, but since there are no higher digits, the carried over 7 becomes a 6 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:9 (with carryover 2).\nTens:4 (carryover 7).\nHundreds:1 (no carryover).\nThousands:7 (carryover 4).\nTen thousands:0 (carryover 0).\nHundred thousands:2 (carryover 7).\nMillions:0. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
