rst, adding 429,734 + 200,000 = 629,734.\n\nThen add the remaining 4,993: 629,734 + 4,993.\n\n629,734 + 4,000 = 633,734.\n\nThen + 993: 633,734 + 993 = 634,727?\n\nWait, now I'm confused. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake in the first method.\n\nWait let me recalculate the first step-by-step addition again.\n\nFirst number: 429,734. Let's break it down digit by digit from right:\n\nDigits: 4 (ones), 3 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 9 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number: 204,993.\n\nDigits: 3 (ones), 9 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 2 (hundred thousands).\n\nNow adding each place:\n\nOnes: 4 +3=7\n\nTens:3+9=12 → 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:7+9=16 +1=17 →7, carryover1.\n\nWait, I think I messed up the hundreds place earlier. Wait, in the hundreds place, first number is 7, second is 9 (since 204,993 has 9 in hundreds place?), yes. So 7+9=16, plus carryover 1 is 17. So write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: first number has 9, second has 4 (since the thousands digit of 204,993 is 4). 9+4=13 +1 (carryover)=14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: first number's ten thousands digit is 2, second is 0. 2+0=2 +1 (carryover)=3. So write 3, no carryover.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 4 +2 =6.\n\nSo putting all together:\n\nOnes:7\n\nTens:2\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:4\n\nTen-thousands:3\n\nHundred-thousands:6\n\nSo the number is 634,727? Wait, let's see:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nHundred thousands: 6,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:7 → 634,727. Wait that's different from the first calculation. Hmm. So where did I go wrong before?\n\nAh, I see. In my first step-by-step, I had the thousands place as 9 (from first number) +0 (from second?), but actually, the thousands digit in the second number (204,993) is 4 (since it's 204,993: the digits are 2 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 3 (ones). So the thousands digit is 4. Therefore, the thousands 