rryover1.\n\nOnes:5+4=9.\n\nWait, hold on, this is different from before. Wait, perhaps I had the hundreds and thousands mixed up earlier.\n\nWait, let\'s redo the digit-wise addition correctly, aligning the columns properly:\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values:\n\nNumber1: 9 4 0 7 7 5\n\nNumber2: 3 3 9 8 7 4\n\nWait, but actually, to align properly, each digit should be in the same column:\n\nHundreds of thousands: 9 (num1) and 3 (num2)\n\nTen thousands:4 and3\n\nThousands:0 and9\n\nHundreds:7 and8\n\nTens:7 and7\n\nOnes:5 and4\n\nSo adding column by column from right to left:\n\nOnes column:5+4=9 → 9\n\nTens column:7+7=14 → write 4, carryover1.\n\nHundreds column:7+8=15 +1=16 →6, carryover1.\n\nThousands column:0+9=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands column:4+3=7 +1=8 →8.\n\nHundred thousands column:9+3=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 from the hundred thousands column becomes the millions place, so the total is 1,270,649.\n\nBut when I added using the other method, I got 1,280,649. Wait, so where is the mistake here?\n\nWait let\'s do the arithmetic again with the second method:\n\n940,775 + 339,874.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 940,775.\n\nAdd 300,000: 940,775 + 300,000 = 1,240,775.\n\nThen add 39,874:\n\nFirst add 30,000: 1,240,775 +30,000 = 1,270,775.\n\nThen add 9,874 to that.\n\n1,270,775 +9,000 = 1,279,775.\n\nThen add 800: 1,279,775 +800 = 1,280,575.\n\nThen add 70: 1,280,575 +70 = 1,280,645.\n\nThen add 4: 1,280,645 +4 =1,280,649.\n\nHmm, so there is a discrepancy. Wait, maybe I miscounted the digits in the second method?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-wise addition. Let me check with a calculator.\n\nLet me compute 940775 + 339874:\n\nFirst, 940,775 + 339,874.\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nStart with the thousands: 900,000 + 300,000 = 1,200,000.\n\nThen 40,000 +39,000 =79,000 → total so far 1,279,000.\n\nThen 700 +800 =1,500 → 1,279,000 +1,500 =1,280,500.\n\nThen 70 +70 =140 → 1,280,500 +140 =1,280,640.\n\nThen 5 +4 =9 → total 1,280,649.\n\nAh, so the correct answer is 1,280,649. That means my initial digit-wise addition had an error. Let\'s see where.\n\nWait, in the thousands place, I had:\n\nThousands column:0 (from first number) +9 (second) plus carryover1 (from hundreds column). So 0+9=9 +1=10. So that gives 0 in thousands place and carryover1 to ten thousands.\n\nThen the ten thousands place:4 +3 +1 (carryover) =8. So that\'s correct.\n\nHundred thousands place:9+3=12 → 2 and carryover1.\n\nThus, the total is 1 (million) 2 (hundred thousands) 8 (ten thousands) 0 (thousands) 6 (hundreds) 4 (tens) 9 (ones). Which is 1,280,649. So where did I go wrong in my initial digit-wise?\n\nWait, I think in my first digit-wise addition I may have miscounted the places. Let me check again step by step:\n\nAfter the thousands place: the thousands digit is 0 +9 +1 (carryover from hundreds) =10 → so thousands digit is 0, carryover1 to ten thousands.\n\nThen ten thousands place:4 (from first number) +3 (second number) +1 (carryover) =8 → ten thousands digit is 8, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place:9+3=12 → so 2 and carryover1. So the hundred thousands digit is 2 and the carryover1 is the millions digit.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\nMillions place:1,\n\nHundred thousands:2,\n\nTen thousands:8,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:9.\n\nSo that\'s 1,280,649. So my first digit-wise addition was correct, but in my first attempt I thought I had 1,270,649 which was a mistake in writing down the digits. So the correct answer is 1,280,649.\n\nTherefore, the final computed sum is 1,280,649.\n'] 93%|#########2| 463/500 [9:17:42<1:13:33, 119.29s/it] 93%|#########2| 464/500 [9:17:59<53:01, 88.37s/it]   
