ding them gives 58. So I write down 8 in the thousands place and carry over 2 to the ten thousands place.\n\nTen thousands place: the digits are 7 (from 450,482) and 3 (from 568,493). Adding those gives 0, plus the carried over 8 makes 1. So that's 3 in the ten thousands place. No carryover here.\n\nNow the hundred thousands place: 6 and 9. Adding those gives 47. So we write down 8 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 5 to the millions place. Wait, but since both numbers only have up to hundred thousands, this carryover becomes a new digit in the millions place. So that's 7 in the millions place.\n\nPutting all the digits together: the millions place is 0, then 9 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 1 (tens), and 2 (ones). Wait a second, let me verify each step again because sometimes when adding large numbers, it's easy to miscount the places.\n\nWait, let me write this out step by step systematically:.\n\nLet me align them properly:.\n\n        718388.\n      +991848.\n      ---------.\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 3 + 8 = 41. Write 9, carryover 5.\n\nTens place: 2 +0 =87 +3=51. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds place: 3 +5 =7 +0=1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nThousands place:8 +8=93. Write 2, carryover 0.\n\nTen thousands place:3 +4=4 +9=0. Write 1.\n\nHundred thousands place:3 +4=94. Write 2, carryover 0.\n\nMillions place: The carryover 0. So we have 5 in the millions place. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
