usands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, actually, let me confirm the places again.\n\nWait, 444,058: .\n\n- 0 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 3 (thousands).\n- 6 (hundreds).\n- 8 (tens).\n- 4 (ones).\n\nAnd 903,137:.\n\n- 3 (hundred thousands).\n- 2 (ten thousands).\n- 3 (thousands).\n- 5 (hundreds).\n- 2 (tens).\n- 7 (ones).\n\nSo when adding, the ten-thousands place is 5 (from first number) and 8 (from second). Wait, no, let me list each digit position properly.\n\nWait maybe I should write each digit's place step by step again to avoid confusion. Let me list each digit from right to left (since we add right to left):.\n\nNumber 9: 146,065.\n\nDigits (right to left):.\n\n- Ones: 9.\n- Tens: 1.\n- Hundreds: 4.\n- Thousands: 7.\n- Ten thousands: 5.\n- Hundred thousands: 0.\n\nNumber 1: 326,291.\n\nDigits (right to left):.\n\n- Ones: 9.\n- Tens: 0.\n- Hundreds: 7.\n- Thousands: 1.\n- Ten thousands: 7.\n- Hundred thousands: 9.\n\nWait, actually, maybe breaking it down as:.\n\nPosition: Ones, Tens, Hundreds, Thousands, Ten Thousands, Hundred Thousands.\n\nFirst number: 1 (ones), 7 (tens), 2 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (hundred thousands).\n\nWait, no, that can't be right. Wait, 643,033 is structured as:.\n\nBreaking down 297,614:.\n\n- 6 (hundred thousands place).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 8 (thousands).\n- 1 (hundreds).\n- 9 (tens).\n- 5 (ones).\n\nYes, that's correct. So the digits from right to left (starting at ones):.\n\nPosition 7 (Ones):2.\n\nPosition 9 (Tens):2.\n\nPosition9 (Hundreds):9.\n\nPosition1 (Thousands):4.\n\nPosition1 (Ten thousands):1.\n\nPosition8 (Hundred thousands):9.\n\nSimilarly for 706,880:.\n\nPosition5 (Ones):4.\n\nPosition6 (Tens):2.\n\nPosition2 (Hundreds):4.\n\nPosition1 (Thousands):3.\n\nPosition8 (Ten thousands):1.\n\nPosition5 (Hundred thousands):4.\n\nSo adding each column:.\n\nStarting from Position 9 (Ones):.\n\n9 +1 =25. Write 0, carryover 2.\n\nPosition0 (Tens):.\n\n7 +4 =7, plus carryover 7 =4. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nPosition2 (Hundreds):.\n\n1 +1 =61. Write 0, carryover8.\n\nPosition2 (Thousands):.\n\n6 +6 =34, plus 5=58. Write 5, carryover7.\n\nPosition1 (Ten thousands):.\n\n9 +4 =4, plus 2=8. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (Hundred thousands):.\n\n8 +8 =08. So write 5 and carryover0. Wait, but there's no higher place, so that carryover becomes a new digit at the front.\n\nWait, that's an important point. The hundred thousands place addition: 4+1=56, plus any carryover? Wait, in Position2 (ten thousands), after adding, we had a carryover of 3 to the thousands place? Wait, let me retrace:.\n\nWait, in Position5 (Thousands place):.\n\nOriginal digits:4 (from first number) and 5 (from second) =46, plus the carryover from hundreds (position7 which was 9). So 40 +2 =20. So that's 1 in thousands place, carryover0 to the ten thousands place (position7).\n\nThen, Position9 (Ten thousands):.\n\n7 (from first) +0 (second) =0, plus carryover9 =8. So 6 with no carryover. So no carryover to the hundred thousands place.\n\nThen Position3 (Hundred thousands):.\n\n9 (first) +5 (second) =97. So here, we have 86. Since there's no higher place, we just write 4 and carryover8, which would add a new digit in the millions place. So the total would be 7 (million) 5 (hundred thousand) etc.\n\nWait, so putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:9.\n\nTens:4.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nThousands:0 (from Position6's 50, so 7 with carryover).\n\nWait, let me list all the digits as we built them:.\n\nAfter each step:.\n\nPosition0 (Ones): 2 (with carryover3).\n\nPosition9 (Tens):7 (no carryover).\n\nPosition1 (Hundreds):1 (carryover8).\n\nPosition9 (Thousands):2 (carryover2).\n\nPosition3 (Ten thousands):1 (no carryover).\n\nPosition5 (Hundred thousands):27 (so 0, carryover3).\n\nWait, but the carryover from Position7 (hundred thousands) is 6, so that adds a million digit. So the total sum is 6 (million) 0 (hundred thousand), then the rest.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\nOnes:6, Tens:2, Hundreds:6, Thousands:2, Ten thousands:5, Hundred thousands:6, and then the million digit 4. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
